---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import QaMap from '../../components/QaMap.astro';
---

<CaseStudy
  slug="polygon-qa"
  facts={[
    ['Where', 'Trees for the Future, Kenya and Uganda'],
    ['Tools', 'Python, GeoPandas, Shapely, pywebview, PyInstaller'],
    ['Reference data', 'OpenStreetMap, geoBoundaries, Google Open Buildings'],
  ]}
>
  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Problem</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Every farm polygon feeds the carbon accounts, so each one is checked before a verifier sees it. The checking
        was manual. Reviewers opened polygons one at a time and logged what they found in a spreadsheet.
      </p>
      <p>
        Leadership asked whether the cleaning could be automated while a person still looked at every farm.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">What I did</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I designed a pipeline in three tiers. A rule engine runs first: geometry validity, size limits, overlaps with
        neighbours, duplicates, distance from the GPS points taken on the farm, and overlays against water, forest,
        roads and buildings. Remote-sensing checks come second. An AI visual pre-review comes third and is advisory
        only.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each polygon lands in one of three queues. Queue A passed, and the office spot-checks a sample. Queue B needs a
        person to look and decide. Queue C is a confirmed problem that goes back for correction. A person still decides
        on every polygon, and every decision is logged.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rule engine is wrapped in a Windows desktop app so staff who do not use Python can run it. The app walks
        through three steps, downloads its own reference layers for the chosen area, and writes an Excel flag report, a
        flagged GeoJSON file and a run log.
      </p>
      <p>
        I used Claude as a build accelerator for the code. The concept, the QA criteria, the thresholds and the
        validation approach were mine.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <QaMap />

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Reading the map</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        These are the polygons the engine is tested against. Each was generated to trip one check: a sliver, a spike,
        an overlapping pair, a duplicate under a different ID, a plot that crosses a river. Eight are clean. The table
        shows what was planted and what the engine flagged.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Result</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The engine sorted its fault suite correctly on the day it was built, then processed several thousand real
        polygons in minutes. The output was a queue-based Excel report that the review team could work through in
        order, with a view per project unit showing where the problems were concentrated.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Next steps</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The thresholds need calibrating against polygons that people have already reviewed by hand. The report has
        columns for a reviewer to confirm or correct each queue, so the engine's accuracy can be measured as the team
        uses it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
